  Okay, what he meant here was the line in the table where A is true and B
is true (11), but switching the context from columns to lines).  He's
saying that in English you can tell from context whether or not "or" is
meant to include "and" or not (although I would argue that it's not
_always_ decidable from context) ("niff" meaning the "not (opposite of) if
and only if", which is the equivalent of xor.)  If you say "We can go to
the movies or the zoo tomorrow", we can assume not both.  But if you say
"would you like carrots or peas with that?" it's not so clear if "both" is
an option or not.

